11
00:41:36,521 --> 00:41:42,521
The King and I is a 1999 American animated
musical film directed by Richard Rich.

12
00:45:23,478 --> 00:45:29,478
It is the only animated feature film produced by Morgan Creek Entertainment.

13
00:49:10,434 --> 00:49:16,434
Loosely based on Richard Rodgers and Oscar Hammerstein II's 1951 stage musical of the same name, it portrays a fictionalized
account of English school teacher Anna Leonowens' historical encounter with king Mongkut of Siam and the royal court.

14
00:52:57,391 --> 00:53:03,391
The voice cast stars Miranda Richardson and Martin Vidnovic as Leonowens and Mongkut,
respectively, with Ian Richardson, Darrell Hammond, and Adam Wylie.

15
00:56:44,347 --> 00:56:50,347
The score, songs, and some of the character names come from the stage musical.

16
01:00:31,304 --> 01:00:37,304
Screenwriters Peter Bakalian, Jacqueline Feather, and David Seidler took creative liberties with the history and
with the source material from the musical in an attempt to make the film palatable to all audiences.

17
01:04:18,260 --> 01:04:24,260
The King and I was released by Warner Bros.

18
01:08:05,217 --> 01:08:11,217
on March 19, 1999, eight months prior to Anna and the King,
a live-action adaptation of the same story.

19
01:11:52,173 --> 01:11:58,173
The film was panned by critics and earned $12 million at the box office against a $25 million budget; its
gross was seen as disappointing compared to that of other animated films released at the time.

20
01:15:39,130 --> 01:15:45,130
The film received five nominations, including the London Critics Circle Film Award for British Actress of the
Year for Richardson and the Golden Reel Award for Best Sound Editing in an Animated Feature.

21
01:19:26,086 --> 01:19:32,086
The film explores themes of forbidden love, musical, based
on play or musical, king, school teacher.

22
01:23:13,043 --> 01:23:19,043
The tone is emotional.
